    <!-- The launch field, light mode. **`AppPalette.canvasLight`, exactly** —
         the same colour the app's own background is, and it has to be the same
         value rather than a near one.

         **This is now a much stronger guarantee than it used to be, not a
         weaker one.** The old note here was a warning: the native window handed
         over to Flutter's first frame with no transition of any kind, so a
         single level of difference was a visible flash, and this value had
         already silently drifted to `#FAFAFA` once. That was a matching problem
         nothing in the build could check.

         `SplashCurtain` paints this same colour as Flutter's first frame and
         then dissolves *only the lockup* off it — the field itself never
         changes. So the background is one continuous colour from the moment
         Android paints the launch window to the moment the app is fully on
         screen, and the only thing that happens is the interface arriving.
         Matching still matters; it is just no longer the only thing standing
         between the app and a flash.

         For a while this was the brand's teal in both modes, which let the mark
         stand on a field designed for it. Following the canvas is the decision
         now, and what it costs is written down in `shoto_brand_mark.dart`: on a
         near-white field the mark's front card is six levels off the ground and
         reads as a very quiet shape. The two teal cards behind it and the
         ribbon are what carry the silhouette there. -->
    <color name="splash_background">#F4F4F4</color>
</resources>
